# SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-3.0-or-later
# akantu-rs CI tooling: valgrind suppressions of the memcheck tier
# (ci/memcheck/run-in-image.sh). Every entry names the third-party library
# that owns the reported memory and why the report is not a defect of
# akantu-rs. Entries are added only from an observed report (quoted below);
# each is anchored at the exact library function that performs the syscall, so
# an uninitialised buffer of akantu-rs code (an MPI payload, a Rust value
# handed to MUMPS / PETSc / Scotch) never matches: payloads leave through
# other functions (mca_btl_tcp_frag_send, the shared-memory BTL, the
# libraries' own kernels) or are read inside the Rust frames.
# Observed on the Debian trixie images (Open MPI 5.0.7, PMIx 2.13.7 "pmix2",
# libevent 2.1.7), valgrind 3.24.0, 2026-11-12.

# PMIx's progress thread sends a message whose pack buffer has uninitialised
# padding bytes (realloc'ed in pmix_bfrop_buffer_extend, written by
# pmix_bfrops_base_pack*). The whole stack, allocation and use, is inside
# libpmix / libevent / libc on the PMIx helper thread (valgrind compares the
# parameter name literally, so the index is the one observed):
#   Syscall param writev(vector[1]) points to uninitialised byte(s)
#     at writev, by pmix_ptl_base_send_handler (libpmix.so.2.13.7),
#     by libevent_core event_base_loop, by start_thread
{
   pmix-ptl-send-handler-uninitialised-pack-padding
   Memcheck:Param
   writev(vector[1])
   ...
   fun:writev
   fun:pmix_ptl_base_send_handler
   ...
}

# Open MPI's TCP BTL sends its connection handshake (a struct on the stack of
# libopen-pal's frame, with padding) with a blocking send. The reported address
# is on the calling thread's stack inside libopen-pal; the akantu frames in the
# report are only the callers that entered MPI progress (PMPI_Test, MPI_Wait):
#   Syscall param socketcall.sendto(msg) points to uninitialised byte(s)
#     at send, by mca_btl_tcp_send_blocking (libopen-pal.so.80.0.5),
#     by libevent_core event_base_loop, by opal_progress, by PMPI_Test, ...
#     Address 0x... is on thread 1's stack
{
   ompi-btl-tcp-send-blocking-handshake-padding
   Memcheck:Param
   socketcall.sendto(msg)
   ...
   fun:send
   fun:mca_btl_tcp_send_blocking
   ...
}
